  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-506575</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consecutive weeks of heavy rains have caused widespread flooding in southern and central Mozambique, forcing families to leave their homes and submerging entire neighborhoods as rivers continue to overflow. On February 5th, the INGD (National Institute for Disaster Management and Risk Reduction) reported that 723,500 people (170,248HH) were affected between January 9th and February 5th, 2026,resulting in 23 deaths, 147 injuries, and 9 missing persons. According to the INGD, 100,382 people were evacuatedand are living in 119 Accommodation Centers (ACs). Projections indicate that up to 800,000 people could be affected if the flooding persists. Gaza province is the most affected, with 443,794 people (92,036 HH), 5 deaths, 38 people injured, 5 missing, and81,495 people displaced and housed in 43 Accommodation Centers.
The floods have caused severe humanitarian impacts, and it is estimated that 392,000 people havebeen displaced as their homes were flooded or destroyed &#x2013; and the number of displaced peoplecontinues to rise. Critical infrastructure, including health facilities, schools, road networks, and essential services, has been extensively damaged. Access remains a major challenge due to flooded and damaged/destroyed roads; and it is estimated that around 5,000 kilometers of roads have been affected, including important stretches linking Maputo to the rest of the country, disrupting supply chains and limiting access to food andservices, the movement of people and goods, fuel, and humanitarian assistance.
Humanitarian needs are rapidly outpacing available resources. Affected communities face significant shortages of shelter and essential household items, food, as well as clean water, sanitation, and hygiene support, increasing the risks of waterborne diseases. Displacement management, health services and protection support are also urgently needed, according (MOZAMBIQUE FLASH APPEAL FLOOD - RESPONSE | 2026, IOM).
In response, Oxfam is requesting&#x20ac;1 million to reinforce life&#x2011;saving assistance, restore livelihoods, and support initial recovery efforts in the most affected provinces (Gaza, Maputo, and Sofala), ensuring that support reaches the communities most in need. This ambition of Oxfam is in line withthe Addendum to the Humanitarian Needs and Response Plan, issued on January 23, which requests $187 million for the next 6 months to support the flood response.
Oxfam remains committed to mobilizing funds to support the response and working together with partnersto protect lives, preserve dignity, and meet the urgent and growing needs of communities affected by the floods.</narrative>
  </description>
